    questions: [
      { question: { en: "What is the kawahiki technique, and how is it typically performed?" }, answers: ["A method of scaling fish underwater", "A skin-pulling technique where one hand pulls the skin back with tension while a nearly flat blade separates it from the flesh", "A method exclusively for removing bones", "A way of cooking fish skin until crisp"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: { en: "Kawahiki involves one hand pulling the skin with steady tension while a nearly flat blade separates flesh from skin in a controlled motion." } },
      { question: { en: "Why do fish like kohada and aji traditionally keep their skin on?" }, answers: ["Their skin cannot be physically removed", "The skin is part of the dish's traditional flavor and visual identity as hikarimono", "Removing skin is considered unsafe on these species", "It saves preparation time only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: { en: "Hikarimono fish like kohada and aji are traditionally served with skin intact as part of their flavor and visual signature." } },
      { question: { en: "For a fish that keeps its skin on, where does the chef's careful handling actually need to happen?" }, answers: ["Nowhere; no special care is needed", "At the earlier scaling stage, working the layer beneath the scales", "Only at the very final plating stage", "Only when removing the head"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: { en: "Since the skin stays on, the careful handling shifts to the scaling stage, addressing the layer directly beneath the scales." } },
      { question: { en: "Why does eel require a different skinning approach than kawahiki on a typical white fish?" }, answers: ["Eel has no skin at all", "Eel skin is unusually tough and slippery, often requiring the eel to be pinned in place before working the blade", "Eel skin comes off on its own without any technique", "Eel is always served whole and never skinned"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: { en: "Eel's tough, slippery skin, coated in natural slime, typically requires pinning the eel in place before a controlled blade motion, unlike the smooth pull of kawahiki." } },
    ],
