    description: { en: "Learn honeboni, the tweezer-based hand-deboning process used to remove fine pin bones a knife alone cannot cleanly extract.", ja: "包丁だけでは綺麗に取り除けない小骨を、骨抜きピンセットで手作業で除去する骨抜きの技術を学びます。", vi: "Hoc honeboni, quy trinh loai bo xuong bang tay voi nhip, dung de go bo nhung xuong dam nho ma dao khong the lam sach gon." },
    goal: { en: "After completing this mission, you will understand why pin bones require hand removal, how honenuki tweezers are used, and how chefs develop the tactile skill to find them.", ja: "このミッションを終えると、なぜ小骨に手作業の除去が必要なのか、骨抜きピンセットの使い方、そして職人が小骨を見つける触覚的な技術をどう身につけるのかを理解できます。", vi: "Sau khi hoan thanh nhiem vu nay, ban se hieu tai sao xuong dam can duoc loai bo bang tay, cach dung nhip honenuki, va cach dau bep phat trien ky nang cam nhan bang xuc giac de tim ra chung." },
    content: {
      en: `Honeboni (骨抜き), which literally means "bone removal," refers to the hand process of locating and extracting the thin, flexible pin bones that remain embedded in a fillet even after the main filleting cut — typically sanmai-oroshi — has separated the flesh from the skeleton. These pin bones are small, often nearly translucent, and follow their own branching pattern through the flesh that doesn't correspond neatly to any single knife cut, which is exactly why a knife alone can never fully remove them: cutting deep enough to catch every bone would destroy far too much good flesh, while a shallower cut leaves several bones behind.

The tool of choice for this work is a pair of fine-tipped tweezers, often referred to as honenuki (骨抜き), essentially a purpose-built version of the same word used for the technique itself. These tweezers have narrow, precisely aligned tips designed to grip a bone that might be barely a millimeter thick without crushing the surrounding flesh or breaking the bone itself, since a broken bone tip left behind defeats the entire purpose of the process. Good honenuki are kept clean, dry, and free of rust for exactly the same reasons a knife edge is cared for — a compromised tool damages delicate work.

Locating the bones is, in practice, more of a tactile skill than a visual one. Many pin bones are difficult or impossible to spot by sight alone, especially once the flesh has been portioned and its surface texture makes small bones easy to miss. Instead, chefs commonly run a fingertip lightly and slowly across the flesh, feeling for a faint point of resistance or a tiny raised ridge where a bone sits just beneath the surface. Once a bone is felt, the tweezers are used to grip it firmly and pull it out along the angle it naturally lies, rather than straight up, since pulling against the bone's natural lie is far more likely to snap it and leave a fragment behind.

This tactile sense — knowing what a hidden bone feels like versus a fold of connective tissue or a natural seam in the muscle — is built almost entirely through repetition, and it's one of the clearer examples in sushi training of a skill that resists being taught purely through explanation. A new apprentice can be told exactly where pin bones tend to run in a given species, but developing the actual fingertip sensitivity to reliably find every one, quickly and without over-handling the fish, takes hundreds of repetitions before it becomes close to automatic. Fish served with even a single missed pin bone is considered a meaningful lapse at a serious counter, since it directly affects the guest's physical experience of eating the piece — which is why honeboni, despite being unglamorous compared to headline cuts, is treated as a genuinely serious skill in its own right.`,
      ja: `骨抜きとは、三枚おろしなどの主な切り身作業の後にも身の中に残る細く柔らかい小骨を、手作業で見つけて取り除く工程である。小骨は身の中で不規則に枝分かれしており、包丁だけでは全てを取り除くことができないため、骨抜きと呼ばれる先端の細いピンセットを使い、骨を潰さず身も傷めずに引き抜く。小骨の位置は視覚だけでは分かりにくいことが多く、指先を身の表面に軽く滑らせてわずかな抵抗や小さな隆起を感じ取る触覚的な技術が重要であり、骨が寝ている向きに沿って引くことで折れを防ぐ。この感覚は説明だけでは身につかず、数百回の繰り返しを通じて養われるものであり、骨が一本でも残っていることは真剣な店では重大な見落としとされる。`,
      vi: `Honeboni la quy trinh dung tay de tim va loai bo nhung xuong dam mong, deo con nam lai trong mieng ca sau khi da qua nhat cat chinh nhu sanmai-oroshi. Nhung xuong nay phan nhanh khong theo mot duong cat nao ca, nen dao khong the lay het duoc ma khong lam hao phi qua nhieu thit ngon, vi vay nguoi ta dung nhip dau nho goi la honenuki de gap va rut chung ra. Viec tim xuong chu yeu dua vao cam giac xuc giac hon la thi giac: dau bep luot nhe dau ngon tay tren mieng ca de cam nhan mot diem can nho hay mot gon nho nhu noi xuong nam duoi be mat, roi dung nhip rut xuong theo huong tu nhien cua no de tranh lam gay. Ky nang cam nhan nay chi hinh thanh qua hang tram lan lap lai, va viec bo lot mot xuong dam duy nhat duoc xem la mot sai sot dang ke tai mot quan an nghiem tuc.`,
    },
    culturalInsight: { en: "Because pin bone removal happens entirely out of the guest's sight, it's a skill judged purely by its absence of failure — a perfectly boneless piece draws no attention at all, while a single missed bone is remembered.", ja: "小骨の除去は完全に客の目に見えないところで行われるため、失敗がないことでのみ評価される技術であり、完全に骨のない一枚は何の注目も引かないが、たった一本の見落としは記憶に残る。", vi: "Vi viec loai bo xuong dam dien ra hoan toan ngoai tam nhin cua khach, day la ky nang duoc danh gia hoan toan qua su vang mat cua sai sot — mot mieng ca khong con xuong hoan hao se khong thu hut chu y nao, nhung chi mot xuong bi bo lot se bi ghi nho." },
    chefTip: { en: "Pull each pin bone along the angle it naturally lies in the flesh rather than straight up — fighting against its natural lie is what causes bones to snap and leave a fragment behind.", ja: "小骨は真上に引くのではなく、身の中で寝ている自然な角度に沿って引くこと — 自然な向きに逆らうと骨が折れて破片が残ってしまう。", vi: "Hay rut moi xuong dam theo goc tu nhien ma no nam trong thit, khong nen keo thang len — chong lai huong tu nhien cua xuong la nguyen nhan khien no bi gay va de lai manh vun." },
    keyTakeaway: { en: "A perfect honeboni job is invisible; a single missed bone is unforgettable.", ja: "完璧な骨抜きは目に見えないが、たった一本の見落とした骨は忘れられない。", vi: "Mot cong viec honeboni hoan hao la vo hinh; nhung chi mot xuong bi bo lot se khong bao gio duoc quen." },
    xp: 100,
